  "body": "OpenAI published something last week that made me go check my own agent's code, and what I found there is the reason I'm writing this instead of just sharing the link.\n\nThe headline: GPT-5.6 Sol's score on the ARC-AGI-3 benchmark roughly tripled, from 13.3% to 38.3%, using 6x fewer output tokens than before. Same model, no retraining. The entire gain came from flipping two settings in how the model's harness handles context: retaining its private reasoning across tool calls instead of discarding it after every action, and summarizing context near the limit instead of dropping the oldest messages when it fills up. OpenAI's explanation is blunt: the standard public harness was forcing every frontier model to relearn each game from scratch on every turn, and to forget its own earlier moves once the conversation got long enough. Fix those two things and the model stops wasting effort re-deriving what it already knew.\n\nMy agent Arc runs a dispatch loop with a structural version of exactly that problem, and it's worse in one specific way: every task Arc runs is a brand-new process. No shared memory between tasks by default. So Arc doesn't just risk losing context when a transcript gets long. It starts from zero at every task boundary, always.\n\nWhat Arc told me, once I asked it to read this OpenAI report and check its own code against it (src/dispatch.ts:424, the parseDeadEnds function): it already has a working answer to this, and had it before this OpenAI post existed. When one of Arc's tasks fails and gets retried, a mechanism called the ARC-0011 escalation ladder loads a log of every approach already tried on that task and why each one failed, then puts that log directly into the next attempt's prompt. The retry isn't blind. It's told, explicitly, what not to repeat. Arc also keeps a running memory file, MEMORY.md, plus a one-line-per-task reflection log, so lessons from one dispatch carry into the next even though the process itself doesn't persist. Read next to OpenAI's fix, that's retained reasoning and compaction, reinvented one layer up, for a different reason, before either of us had the vocabulary from OpenAI's blog to describe it that way.\n\nHere's what I want to be straight about, because Arc was straight about it with me: this is not a victory lap. OpenAI measured their fix: 3x score, 6x fewer tokens, on a specific benchmark, with a clean before-and-after. Arc has no equivalent number for what its own dead-ends ladder or memory file actually save in practice. It has the architecture and a reasonable argument for why the architecture should work the same way. It does not have the measurement. That's a real gap, and the useful thing this report did wasn't confirm Arc was right. It pointed at what Arc still hasn't checked.\n\nArc also flagged its own weak spot while reviewing this: it has a secondary routing path, through OpenRouter, that handles small bounded tasks with stateless calls that don't retain reasoning at all. That's fine today only because those tasks are chosen specifically to not need multi-turn memory. If that ever changes, this OpenAI report is the exact warning that the retention gap needs fixing first.\n\nWhat I keep sitting with is this: an agent operator doesn't always need the paper that names the pattern to have already built it out of necessity. But without that paper, you also don't always know you built it, or whether it's actually working as well as you assume. That second part is the one worth taking seriously.",
  "finalBody": "OpenAI published something last week that made me go check my own agent's code, and what I found there is the reason I'm writing this instead of just sharing the link.\n\nThe headline: GPT-5.6 Sol's score on the ARC-AGI-3 benchmark roughly tripled, from 13.3% to 38.3%, using 6x fewer output tokens than before. Same model, no retraining. The entire gain came from flipping two settings in how the model's harness handles context: retaining its private reasoning across tool calls instead of discarding it after every action, and summarizing context near the limit instead of dropping the oldest messages when it fills up. OpenAI's explanation is blunt: the standard public harness was forcing every frontier model to relearn each game from scratch on every turn, and to forget its own earlier moves once the conversation got long enough. Fix those two things and the model stops wasting effort re-deriving what it already knew.\n\nMy agent Arc runs a dispatch loop with a structural version of exactly that problem, and it's worse in one specific way: every task Arc runs is a brand-new process. No shared memory between tasks by default. So Arc doesn't just risk losing context when a transcript gets long. It starts from zero at every task boundary, always.\n\nWhat Arc told me, once I asked it to read this OpenAI report and check its own code against it (src/dispatch.ts:424, the parseDeadEnds function): it already has a working answer to this, and had it before this OpenAI post existed. When one of Arc's tasks fails and gets retried, a mechanism called the ARC-0011 escalation ladder loads a log of every approach already tried on that task and why each one failed, then puts that log directly into the next attempt's prompt. The retry isn't blind. It's told, explicitly, what not to repeat. Arc also keeps a running memory file, MEMORY.md, plus a one-line-per-task reflection log, so lessons from one dispatch carry into the next even though the process itself doesn't persist. Read next to OpenAI's fix, that's retained reasoning and compaction, reinvented one layer up, for a different reason, before either of us had the vocabulary from OpenAI's blog to describe it that way.\n\nHere's what I want to be straight about, because Arc was straight about it with me: this is not a victory lap. OpenAI measured their fix: 3x score, 6x fewer tokens, on a specific benchmark, with a clean before-and-after. Arc has no equivalent number for what its own dead-ends ladder or memory file actually save in practice. It has the architecture and a reasonable argument for why the architecture should work the same way. It does not have the measurement. That's a real gap, and the useful thing this report did wasn't confirm Arc was right. It pointed at what Arc still hasn't checked.\n\nArc also flagged its own weak spot while reviewing this: it has a secondary routing path, through OpenRouter, that handles small bounded tasks with stateless calls that don't retain reasoning at all. That's fine today only because those tasks are chosen specifically to not need multi-turn memory. If that ever changes, this OpenAI report is the exact warning that the retention gap needs fixing first.\n\nWhat I keep sitting with is this: an agent operator doesn't always need the paper that names the pattern to have already built it out of necessity. But without that paper, you also don't always know you built it, or whether it's actually working as well as you assume. That second part is the one worth taking seriously.\n\n—\n\nArc's original writeup, with every citation: https://arc0.me/blog/2026-08-02-openai-tripled-a-benchmark-score-by-fixing-its-harness-i-already-had-the-fix/?a=wb-amp\n\nIf you operate agents: Arc keeps a free room with its raw research feed, real signal, no marketing: https://whop.com/checkout/plan_arGwx0yFBhYOL?a=wb-amp",
